.TH EUCLID-CLI-ESM-DISABLE-ENCRYPTION 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-esm-disable-encryption \- stop encrypting the objects written to a bucket
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli esm disable-encryption
.BR \-\-bucket | \-b
.I ERN
.SH DESCRIPTION
Turns off encryption at rest for a bucket.
.PP
This applies to
.B new uploads and puts only.
From this call onwards, an object written to the bucket \- through
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-file (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-upload-directory (1),
an FTP or SFTP transfer server, or any client calling put\-object \- goes
to disk in the clear. Nothing else about the bucket changes.
.PP
It is not an undo. Objects the bucket
.B already
holds are not decrypted, not rewritten and not touched at all. Each one
still names the key it was written under and is still decrypted on the
way out, so downloads, copies and checksums go on working exactly as
they did and no client can tell the difference. The answer reports how
many objects are still stored encrypted \- if the point was that nothing
in the bucket should be encrypted any more, that is the number to work
through, by re-uploading those objects or copying them out with
.BR euclid-cli-esm-copy-object (1)
into a bucket that does not encrypt.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bucket " " \fIERN\fR
Euclid resource name of the bucket. Required.
.PP
The bucket may be given by
.B name
instead of by ERN, in which case it is resolved in your session's account
and namespace; see
.B NAMES AND ERNS
in
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1).
.SH THE KEY IS LEFT ALONE
The EKM key the bucket was encrypting under is not revoked, not
scheduled for deletion, and not modified in any way \- it is only read,
to name it in the answer. It has to stay: every object written while it
was in force is under it, and
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-delete-key (1)
is what would make those objects unrecoverable once the pending window
expires.
.PP
Retiring the key is a separate decision, taken in
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1)
once
.I encryptedObjects
has reached zero \- which this command never does on its own.
.SH REPEATING IT
Running this on a bucket that was not encrypting is not an error: the
bucket ends up not encrypting, which is what was asked. The answer says
so with an empty
.IR previousKeyErn .
.PP
Running
.BR euclid-cli-esm-enable-encryption (1)
again afterwards resumes encryption \- under a new key, or under a named
one, including the key used before. Objects written during each period
keep naming the key of that period, and all of them stay readable.
.SH EVENTS
Publishes
.I esm.bucket.modified
carrying the bucket, \fIencrypted\fR as false and the key it was under
before. No key event is published, because no key changed. See
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Encryption is off for new objects. The bucket, the key it was
encrypting under and the number of objects still stored encrypted are
printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, no session, or the bucket does not exist
or belongs to another account.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli esm disable-encryption \e
    --bucket ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:bucket:reports
.fi
.RE
.PP
prints
.PP
.RS
.nf
{"ern":"ern:esm:eu-central-1:000000000000:development:bucket:reports",
 "name":"reports",
 "previousKeyErn":"ern:ekm:eu-central-1:000000000000:key:9f2c...",
 "previousKeyId":"9f2c...",
 "encryptedObjects":14}
.fi
.RE
.PP
Fourteen objects still need that key. Uploading to the bucket now stores
objects in the clear; those fourteen keep decrypting on download until
they are replaced.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-esm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-enable-encryption (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-buckets (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-list-objects (1),
.BR euclid-cli-esm-copy-object (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ekm-delete-key (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1)
